#
# Mapping (custom Nord-derived shades, not the canonical Nord palette):
# Decode source indices with the standard xterm palette: ANSI 0-15, the 6x6x6
# cube 16-231, and grayscale 232-255. Actual terminal ANSI colors may differ.
# Choose anchors by source HSV: saturation < 0.12 uses nord0-6; otherwise hue
# [0,15)/[345,360) uses nord11, [15,45) nord12, [45,70) nord13,
# [70,165) nord14, [165,190) nord7, [190,255) nord8-10, [255,345) nord15.
# Within a family choose the nearest anchor by Euclidean Oklab distance.
# Blend encoded sRGB channels: round(0.80*anchor + (1-0.80)*source), using
# Python round (nearest integer, ties to even). Reuse the same result everywhere,
# except pale gray foregrounds (216,221,230), (211,216,225), (206,211,220):
# multiply each channel by 0.55 and round. Backgrounds retain the original blend.
# Yellow (nord13-anchored) foregrounds use round(blended channel * 0.90).
# Calculations use Python floats; exact decimal arithmetic may round differently.
# Usage: GNU dircolors/ls and a truecolor terminal are required. TERM patterns
# do not detect truecolor support. Preview from the repository root:
# (eval "$(dircolors -b src/dir_colors_light)"; ls --color=always -CF .)
# On macOS with Homebrew coreutils, use gdircolors and gls instead.
# Intended background: Snow Storm (nord6). Some accents have low contrast;
# readability is not certified. Default and attribute-only rules inherit terminal
# colors; loading this file does not change the terminal theme.
# GNU ls matches literal suffixes, not regexes. Inherited regex-looking keys are
# retained as literals. File types and permissions can override suffix colors.

